[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a minimal, copy-and-paste-ready example that shows how to:

1. **Add a raster-DEM source** (any source that returns 16-bit elevation tiles – e.g. Mapbox Terrain-RGB, Open-Topography, or your own MBTiles/XYZ service).  
2. **Create a `hillshade` layer** that draws shaded relief from that DEM.  

You can drop the code into an existing MapLibre GL JS map or start a fresh page.

---

## 1. Full HTML / JS example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from raster-DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Base map (optional – you can use any style you like)
        "osm": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: "osm-basemap",
          type: "raster",
          source: "osm"
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Add the raster-DEM source
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Example using Mapbox Terrain-RGB (public demo tiles, no token needed)
  // Replace the URL with your own DEM tiles if you have them.
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      // The URL must return 256-pixel tiles with 16-bit elevation encoded as Terrain-RGB.
      // If you host your own tiles, use the same pattern: .../{z}/{x}/{y}.png
      tiles: [
        'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'
        // or: 'https://example.com/tiles/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,
      // Optional: if your DEM tiles are in a different projection (e.g. EPSG:3857) you can set `scheme: "tms"` or `bounds`.
      // scheme: "tms",
      // maxzoom: 14,
      // minzoom: 0,
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Add the hillshade layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'dem',
      // The hillshade layer does **not** need a source-layer name because raster-DEM sources have a single layer.
      paint: {
        // Default values are shown; tweak them to taste.
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315, // direction of light source (degrees clockwise from north)
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,          // vertical exaggeration (1 = true elevation)
        'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000000',
        'hillshade-shadow-color-transition': {duration: 0},
        'hillshade-highlight-color': '#ffffff',
        'hillshade-accent-color': '#bbbbbb',
        // You can also control opacity:
        // 'hillshade-opacity': 0.7
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Add source** (`type: 'raster-dem'`) | Informs MapLibre that the tiles contain elevation data encoded as 16-bit Terrain-RGB (or any other 16-bit DEM format). The source can be any XYZ/WMTS endpoint that serves such tiles. |
| **Add layer** (`type: 'hillshade'`) | The only layer type that can consume a raster-DEM source. It automatically computes slope, aspect, and shading on the client side. |
| **Paint properties** | Control the visual appearance of the hillshade (light direction, exaggeration, colors, opacity). All are optional – the defaults give a classic “relief map” look. |

---

## 2. Key points & optional tweaks

| Topic | Details |
|-------|---------|
| **DEM tile format** | MapLibre expects the elevation to be encoded as **Terrain-RGB** (R × 256 × 256 + G × 256 + B − 32768) or any 16-bit raster where the red channel holds the elevation. If you have a GeoTIFF-based MBTiles, you can serve it as PNG/TIFF with the same encoding. |
| **CORS** | The tile server must allow cross-origin requests (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain). Otherwise the hillshade will fail to load. |
| **Zoom limits** | Set `maxzoom`/`minzoom` on the source if your DEM tiles only exist for a subset of zoom levels. |
| **Performance** | Hillshade is computed on the GPU, but large DEM tiles (high `maxzoom`) can increase memory usage. If you only need hillshade at low zoom, limit the source to `maxzoom: 12` (or whatever matches your tiles). |
| **Combining with a terrain 3-D map** | If you also want a 3-D terrain surface (`map.setTerrain({source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5})`), you can keep the same DEM source for both the terrain and the hillshade layer. The hillshade will still render as a 2-D overlay, which often looks good under a 3-D view. |
| **Styling** | You can animate the light direction (e.g., with `setPaintProperty` in a `requestAnimationFrame` loop) to create a “sun-move” effect. |
| **Alternative sources** | If you have a **vector DEM** (e.g., Mapbox Terrain v2), you cannot use `hillshade`; you would need to generate raster-DEM tiles first or use the `terrain` API for 3-D only. |

---

## 3. Minimal style-JSON snippet (if you prefer to edit a style file)

If you already have a style JSON and just want to inject the DEM source and hillshade layer, add the following objects:

```json
{
  "sources": {
    "dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "tiles": [
        "https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY"
      ],
      "tileSize": 256
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "hillshade",
      "type": "hillshade",
      "source": "dem",
      "paint": {
        "hillshade-illumination-direction": 315,
        "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.5
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Insert the `dem` source alongside any other sources, and place the `hillshade` layer **after** your base raster/vector layers but **before** any label layers you want to stay on top.

---

## 4. Quick checklist before you run

1. **DEM tiles reachable** – open one tile URL in the browser; you should see a PNG/TIFF image (not a 404).  
2. **CORS headers** – check the network tab; the response must include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.  
3. **Correct source type** – `type: "raster-dem"` (not `"raster"`).  
4. **MapLibre version** – hillshade works from the very first releases, but the paint properties shown above require **v5.0+** (most recent versions are fine).  
5. **Optional token** – if you use a commercial provider (Mapbox, Maptiler, etc.), replace `YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY` with a valid key.

---

### TL;DR

```js
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  tiles: ['https://example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'dem',
  paint: {
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5
  }
});
```

That’s all you need to get a shaded-relief overlay on your MapLibre GL JS map. Happy mapping!
